Date: Sun, 16 May 1993 01:14:23 -0700 (PDT)
From: szabo@techbook.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: TECH: Numerical Speculation

Hal Finney:
> Would there be a difference between a market which was
> absolutely positive that Clinton had a 50% chance of winning, versus one
> which was uncertain and considered Clinton's chances to be in the 10-80%
> range I mentioned above?

The uncertainty market players have about the 50% is reflected in the 
price volatility, or the standard deviation in the price per unit time,
assuming the market players are rational.   (Some successful traders
like George Soros think that irrational psychology plays a major
role in real-world market volatility, but that's another story). The 
volatility in Drake Equation idea futures will be quite high, since one 
new piece of relevant information can change the Drake estimate by orders 
of magnitude.  Just so we could chart it, I'd make the Drake market 
log-scale instead of linear.

Players can bet on the volatility itself by purchasing a "straddle", or 
combination of call option and put option on a futures options market.  
(There are also ways to synthesize a straddle using loans,
short & long positions if there is no options market).  The payoff of 
a straddle looks like a "V": it pays off well if the underlying
security's price (in this case, the futures price) goes way up 
or down, and loses money if the price is stable.  Players can also 
construct a "butterfly spread" which is a bet paying off if the market 
has lower than expected volatility.

The volatility can also change over time.  If the volatility
has been decreasing over a long period of time, this means the
market is converging on an answer.  Not necessarily the most
correct answer; it is common to converge prematurely
on suboptimal answers.  This also happens with science (paradigms),
evolution (puntuated equilibria), etc.  The rate of volatility
convergence lets us to estimate the range of probabilities 
of the "correct" answer, although (just as with scientific theories)
there is no iron-clad guaruntee that the answer is ultimately correct.

The Drake equation market is not zero-information; rather much of the
information comes in the form of constraints.  Upper and lower bounds
can be set on components of the Drake Equation, and also on the
result by using observations beyond the scope of those components
(eg the Fermi Paradox).  It is especially important to 
set bounds on the most uncertain components of the Drake Equation, the 
chances of life originating and the chances of intelligence evolving.
The market player, having determined such constraints, however large
they may be, can use them to place bets on future Drake market volatility.

> Does it even make sense to speak of being
> certain about a probability of a one-time event?

We can be very nearly certain in some cases.  The expiration of an option
is a one-time event: it is either worth its strike price minus the
underlying asset's current price, or it is worth nothing.  A 
50%-out-of-the-money put option on the day of expiration is almost 
certainly worth zero, since the chances of a stock dropping 50% in one 
day are near zero.

We can estimate degree of future uncertainty by observing related events.
The volatility throughout the '92 U.S. Presidential election market can be 
determined post-hoc.  By comparing it to poll figures, we can derive a 
function from poll figure volatility to election market volatility, and use
that to construct volatilties for hypothetical past election markets,
and in turn use those to project the volatility of future election
markets.  That's one possible method; there might be several
other ways to get an estimate of future election market volatilities.

Hal Finney (in previous article):
> I take two coins and lay them on the table either heads or tails,
> choosing how each will lie.  I then cover them, and we run a betting
> market on whether each coin is heads or tails, as well as the total
> number of heads exposed.  Later I will reveal the coins and we will
> settle the betting.

Like the other examples you have cited, this is not a zero-info market.
I would *not* assume independent odds; rather I would assume 
dependencies and patterns. This effect would become more pronounced
the more coins are involved, eg the probability of 10 heads in 10 coins
is n*1/2^10, p(20 heads, 20 coins) is m*1/2^20, and m>n>1.   Likewise
the likelihood of other low-entropy patterns (eg equal numbers of heads and
tails) is greater.  Furthermore, the outcome of the first few coins will 
highly influence the probabilities for the remaining coins, since humans 
are prone to repeat patterns; even the smartest person's choices would 
make a very poor random generator compared to coin tossing.  Here we
can invoke the frequency-counting work in information theory.  

Of course, this presupposes that you are choosing in your head, not from 
a written or memorized computer-generated sequence.   We could back
up and bet on even that issue; ie place bets on the entropy of the
coin sequence, and only starting betting for or against patterns once
a lack of entropy has been established.  Only if the sequence is
completely patternless will I admit the possibility of a zero-information
market (while still holding out the possibility of future cryptanalysis :-)

