Date:     Sun, 16 May 93 8:47:07 GMT
From: starr@genie.slhs.udel.edu
Subject: The Libernet Administration Experience

Carol Moore cites libernet as a good example of a well-administered list.
I don't think that its administration is conducive to the extropians list.

As is, Libernet is split into two lists that can be subscribed to separately:
a sort of newswire, for informational, descriptive posts only, and one
for debate.  The result of this is that the former now has a fairly high
signal to noise ratio, and probably is the de facto libertarian wire service
for lack of superior competition.

This is all to the good.  However, designation of a list for the express
purpose of discussion, debate, argument, etc., has had more mixed returns.
Since the old, unified list had a policy of discouraging flames to some
extent, what has happened is somewhat like I'm told London's Hyde Park is:
flames, debates, and discussions have come out of the woodwork.  This can't
possibly have increased the signal to noise ratio!  Basically, people have
given up any inhibitions about posting well.

And I can't say that I haven't contributed to the noise.  I greatly prefer
our complaint-driven moderation system to one in which rules are enforced
at the discretion of the moderator to no moderation at all, and our one-
list-fits-all format is preferable to bifurcation.

In a way, what I really prefer is how it's the readers who are driving
the list administration - not the central authority.  Our central authority
is restricted to dispute resolution of last resort.  The plans for new
list software will permit users to choose what they will read, too.  And,
if Carol's idea is adopted, who will be excluded from getting posts, too,
will be decided by the posters themselves.

I'm quite content with this.  I can't see why so many complain about it so
much aside from those who want to break the rules.

