HEADLINE: New Laws Needed to Prevent Computer Privacy Violations

BODY:

LINDA WERTHEIMER, Host: Computers, even private ones, are increasingly
linked by electronic systems for sharing information and that trend is
being encouraged by, among others, Vice President Al Gore who wants to
see a national data network, a kind of information highway, linking
the country.  

But the more computers are connected, the more concerns individual
users have about privacy - how to keep 'hackers' from following that
information highway right into professional secrets and personal
accounts.  And those concerns are shared by the federal government,
which wants data protected from unauthorized eyes but also wants its
own way in, just in case.  Computer consultant Fred Davis says there
are software programs intended to protect computers but the government
is considering a whole new system of security.

FRED DAVIS, Computer Consultant: There are a number of incription
programs that are commercially sold.  The most popular is based on the
data incription standard or DES, which was designed about 20 years ago
and is probably inadequate but we don't know because the government
won't let us look at the algorithm that's used in that standard.  Most
computer hackers think that it's probably bad, out-of- date and needs
a new fix and in fact, the government is now thinking of ways to come
up with a new fix.  They want to have a skeleton key so they can
unlock our codes on us.

WERTHEIMER: 'Skeleton key?' Explain that.

Mr. DAVIS: Well, the idea with any kind of computer incription is that
you put in some kind of password or code that essentially acts like a
key and locks your information and unless someone else knows that
special code, they can't unlock the data file.

WERTHEIMER: But what you're talking about is the government reserving
for itself the skeleton key?

Mr. DAVIS: What they want to do- at first they said, 'Hey, let's just
get a skeleton key that will unlock any code.' That met with howls of
protest.  The idea that anybody could get a hold of this key and it
could fall into an information terrorist's hands and they could look
at any of your credit reports or private information, was scary.  So
the concept was that the skeleton key would be broken in half,
essentially and half of it would be kept by one public agency and the
other half by another public agency.  Then when the government wanted
to get a court order or a search warrant to intercept a transmission
or unlock a file, they'd have to request both halves of the key, glue
them together and then they'd be able to unlock everything.  

The thing that's spooky about this is you really fear that they'll
misuse it.  The potential for abuse is tremendous, not only by the
government but by an illegal hacker who figures out how to make a
skeleton key on their own, and it's very unsettling.  If you feel that
you're getting protected by an incription chip or some other encoding
technology, it give you a false sense of security, especially if
anybody can get this skeleton key and open it up and look at your
secrets.  Certainly for businesses this would be very scary - for
trade secrets, the secret ingredient in Coke and all of those things.

WERTHEIMER: How easy is it right now to break into a computer?

Mr. DAVIS: Well, for a talented hacker, it's pretty easy,
unfortunately.  It depends on the level of security on the system
they're trying to break into. Banks and credit care places and
financial institutions have very good security. Corporations have fair
security and individual people usually have no security so somebody
could probably get into your personal computer pretty easily. Anytime
the information is attached to a wire, people can sneak in over that
wire electronically and look at what's in your computer.

WERTHEIMER: Do you think a computer hacker would be able to get to
'the big key?'

Mr. DAVIS: Well, my experience is that computer hackers are usually
smarter than the people in the government and there's not been a
system that they've been unable to break into, given enough time and
patience.  And plus they're- a lot of these folks, they're all over
the world.  So if you have a whole team of some of the world's
brightest criminal minds working on this, I don't think it would be
too long before they could break into the thing.

WERTHEIMER: Do we need a whole new set of laws here, Fred, to deal
with a whole new kind of privacy.

Mr. DAVIS: Boy, I think that is absolutely critical.  I mean, for
example, right now there are a lot of laws to protect the U.S. Mail.
If I go into your mailbox and open a letter and read it, that's
clearly illegal.  It's not clearly illegal to do that with electronic
mail.  So as more and more of our personal and business communications
get converted into electronic mail, all sorts of people- you don't
even need a search warrant to go read this stuff.  So we need a lot
of, I think, very stringent privacy laws to protect our natural right
to privacy.  

I mean, not even, you know, like I said, you don't even need a search
warrant to go read people's electronic mail.  There have been a lot of
class-action suits from employees of companies where their boss has
gone in and read all their E-mail and they said, 'Hey, there's nothing
illegal about it, so we just want to find out what you guys are up
to.' One of the feelings in the computer industry is that privacy and
incription products and security products are probably going to be one
of the very hot software categories over the next couple of years.
Some hackers have gotten together and created their own incription
standard they call, pretty good privacy, or PGP, and that's available
right now and other products like that will probably come out.

You know, I think that people should reject the government's proposed
key and just come up with their own.  There are ways, if you don't
make the skeleton key a potential, you can lock people out of using
it.  I mean, you can come up with a very secure code.  Once you have
an ability to break it with a skeleton key, that's where the whole
thing unravels.

WERTHEIMER: Thank you very much.  Computer analyst Fred Davis.  His
latest book is the Windows 3.1 Bible.  He joined us from San
Francisco.

