>are probably fairly efficient designs.  I carefully excused myself
>from having to include these in my estimates by specifying that I was
>talking about a thinking machine without sensory apparatus more
>sophisticated than a TTY connection.

 I believe that a human mind without the visualizing parts would be missing
a lot more than just sensory input, and I doubt whether such a mind could
even posess rudimentary sanity. It is not clear how a thinking machine
could work without reproducing significant portions of these processes.

 Why is that we use phrases such as "I see what you mean" rather than
"I hear what you mean"? 

 The most compelling testimony for my viewpoint comes from a quote in
Jacques Hadamard's book "The Psychology of Invention in the Mathematical
Field", from Albert Einstein:
 "The words or the language, as they are written or spoken, do not seem to
play any role in my mechanism of thought. The psychical entities which seem
to serve as elements in thought are certain signs and more or less clear
images which can be "voluntarily" reproduced and combined. ...
The above mentioned elements are, in my case, of visual and some of muscular
type. Conventional words or other signs have to be sought for laboriously
only in a secondary stage ..."

 Similar testimonies have been reported from other top mathematical scientists.
 While images may play a less important role in the thought of an average
person, I think that there is a lot of image processing that occurs sub-
consciously which is used in everday commonsense knowledge.

[ Carl apparently left on vacation before I found time to respond, so this
conversation may not amount to much. Why is it that the most interesting
discussions on this list start near the end of semesters, when I am too busy
to give them adequate attention?]
