 dgordon@crow.omni.co.jp  (David Gordon) writes:
>  What lack of diversity destabilizes an economy? Daedalus reckons it
>  is the single currency.  The EC's goal of a single currency is thus
>  exactly wrong, as you might expect.  Instead, it should achieve
>  financial unification by making all its currencies legal tender in
>  every member state.

 Competition between currencies would certainly have a stabilizing effect.
I don't think it would create diversity, any more than it has created
diversity in the credit card market, because of the transaction costs
of converting between currencies, and because there's not much diversity
in the functions that people want currency to serve.

 The one problem with the proposal that you mention is that making many
currencies legal tender is very different from creating freely competing
currencies. Legal tender laws put a one-sided obligation on creditors
to accept whatever currency that debtors want to give them. This is what
causes Gresham's "Law", with debtors always getting rid of the least
valuable currency, with the result that the worst currency circulates the
most. What is needed is the abolition of legal tender laws.

