Subject: PHIL: The Utility of Mathematics

Eric Raymond says:
> Unfortunately, it turns out that there is more than one way to
> axiomatize set theory.  In particular, there are at least four major
> different combinations of assumptions about infinite sets that lead
> to mutually exclusive set theories (the Axiom of Choice or its
> negation; the Continuum Hypothesis or its negation).

In fact it's even 'worse' than that.

The Axiom of Choice has a choosable true/ false status.  The Axiom of
Choice says you can always define a procedure for selecting a single
element of any set.  The negation simply says this in not always so. 
Interestingly the Communist World never accepted the Axiom of Choice. 
To them it seemed reasonable that there might exist sets for which no
procedure could select a single element.  If all animals are equal, how
do you tell one from the other?  Only a Menshevik-dog, Capitalist-pig
would declare that all sets contain special elements :-)  As result they
had to 'prove' theorems without recourse to the Axiom of Choice and so
Eastern Bloc maths was always at a tangent to Western maths.

The Continuum Hypothesis says you have no transfinite number lurking
between aleph-null and aleph-one (= 2^aleph-null).  But there are an
infinite number of ways of negating this.  By fiat you can declare the
existence of any finite number of extra non-Cantorian transfinites
between aleph-null and aleph-one.  Hence an aleph-null (not just four)
number of different, orthogonal axiom bases emerge to confound and
shatter the old Platonic World into ... Many Worlds :-)

And that's without mentioning the Generalised Continuum Hypothesis which
has at least aleph-one negations....

> In a negative way, though, a Platonist had the last laugh.  Kurt Godel
> threw a spanner in the Formalist program of axiomatization when he
> showed that any axiom system powerful enough to include the integers
> would have to be either inconsistent (yielding contradictions) or
> incomplete (too weak to decide the truth or falsehood of some
> assertions in the system).  
> 
> And that is more or less where things stand today.  Mathematicians
> know that any attempt to put forward mathematics as a-priori knowledge
> about the universe must fall afoul of numerous paradoxes and
> impossible choices about  what axiom system describes "real"
> mathematics.  They've been reduced to hoping that the standard
> axiomatizations are not inconsistent but incomplete, and wondering
> uneasily what contradictions or unprovable theorems are waiting
> discovery out there, lurking like landmines in the noosphere.

I've never understood all the collective wailing and gnashing of teeth
about Godel.  I don't see it as undermining mathematics or the a-priori
knowledge ideal.  The way I interpret Godel is every axiom base is
incomplete in that you will always encounter undecidable theorems which
are neither true nor false within it.  Instead of shrinking in horror
from this I accept it as meaning that the adoption of the unprovable
theorem or one of its negations, as an axiom, will lead to an extension
of mathematics.  Within each of the extended axiom set new theorems,
including undecidable ones, are generated.  And the process continues
without end.  As for which choice of this infinite dimensional axiom
base is "true" - why they all are!  A or ~A, depending on the situation. 
"Real" mathematics is the sum of all the alternatives.  Like an
infinitely long fractal branching tree in axiom/theorem space.  

The Tree of a-priori Truth has many branches.  Godel has guaranteed that
mathematics will never end.  Praise be to Godel.

