aleph-one is not defined as 2^aleph-naught
aleph-one is the first cardinal following aleph-naught
there is no such thing as "non-Cantorian" transfinites
Continuum Hypothesis states:
aleph-one = continuum

by definition, continuum = 2^aleph-one 
or not by definition, if you choose to define continuum as cardinality of reals

The Eastern Bloc mathematicians did not deny the Axiom of Choice. 
Study Banach-Tarski paradox: if AC holds, then I can take a sphere,
split it into five pieces, take two of them, rotate and translate, and get
the same sphere back
then take the remaining three, rotate them and translate, and get the same
sphere back

This is in response to the post by Michael Clive Price on AC and CH --
please learn those before you post on them, just learn what they say.

By the way, AC has a version which is not as counterintuitive as the ones
stated: for any class of sets, the Cartesian product of the class is
nonempty as long as all of the sets are nonempty. The problem with AC is
not that you can pick "special elements", but the concept of actual
infinity -- the very same thing Carl Friedrich Gauss so vigorously
opposed. 

Jan Mycielski of UofColorado created a system of axioms of set theory
without actual infinity, all of calculus works just fine in it.

As long as there is no actual infinity, all of the so called problems
disappear. It is the fact that the set of integers is infinite, and so
inclusion of actual infinity in the model, which causes Godel's stuff to
work.

Axiom of Choice and Continuum Hypothesis are fun to play with, but let's
not get too serious about it. Gauss was right.

