To use a metaphor such as Eric likes (as in "and now the dragon is
half slain"), the concept of knowable absolute truth in the
formalist/Russell manner got nuked a long time ago. The latest
deepening of the crater was Chaitin's 1987 proof about the
randomizness of mathematics at all levels (no, not the mundane stuff
about chaos, attractors, blah, blah)...Chaitin proved arithmetic
itself, the rock of mathematics, is itself fundamentally random! As
he put it, "God not only plays dice in quantum mechanics, but even
with the whole numbers."

This doesn't mean knowledge is formless, provided form is defined in
operational effectiveness terms. Reality is a landscape with many
maps, maps of known truths (modulo some axioms), maps of undecidabel
propositions, and maps (as Chaitin showed) of questions which have
answers but which cannot be formally represented in any axiom system.

Some of these maps we mostly have sketched out, some maps we have
glimpses of, and some maps we suspect to exist but will never see. 

The dragon wasn't slain...he saw what was coming and crawled back
into his egg and pulled the bits of shell around him.

(Sorry for metaphorical excess!)


....However, one can still be a Platonist, albeit of a slightly
different form than Plato would've imagined. Godel himself remained a
kind of Platonist.

Now of course Platonism is not the naive picture of "Circles" and
"Open Sets" living some existence out in some abstract space of
Reality. It is more the notion that the space of provable theorems and
askable questions is out there waiting for us to explore it, to map
it, to mark some regions with "Here be dragons!" signs (to continue
Eric's metaphor), and to see "knowledgequakes" occur as our mental
framework suddenly shifts and we see new and perhaps better glimpses
of this Reality.

The relevance of these results for Extropianism in general and our
place in the Universe in particular is left as an exercise.



